                <pb facs="0912" n="878" xml:id="pg878"/>

                <head>413. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On November 13, Ambassador Slim met with Under Secretary Dillon to discuss the
                    U.S. decision not to grant Tunisia a loan for the Oued Nebana project. Dillon
                    assured Slim the United States had not made a final decision and was
                    reevaluating the project in light of new information. He also explained the
                    technical and legal difficulties it presented. A memorandum of this conversation
                    is in Department of State, Central Files, 772.5–MSP/11–1659. Its substance was
                    sent to Tunis in telegram 633, November 16. (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Ibid.</hi>)</p>
                <p>Clarence Randall, Chairman of the Council on Foreign Economic Policy, arrived in
                    Tunis on December 7, where he and his party were briefed on the Tunisian
                    economic situation, December 7–8, by the Embassy staff. Reports on his trip are
                    in Eisenhower Library, CFEP Records, Office of the Chairman, and in a Special
                    Staff Note, January 12; <hi rendition="#italic">ibid.,</hi> Whitman File,
                    Eisenhower Diaries.</p>
            </div>
